"How Good is Post-Hoc Watermarking With Language Model Rephrasing? (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16904v1)\nciteturn6471view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6469view1\",\"lineno\":192}); Total lines: 729\nL171: Rephrasing is done chunk by chunk of $L$ tokens. For each chunk, we generate $m$ candidates $\\{\\tilde{y}^{(1)},\\dots,\\tilde{y}^{(m)}\\}$ without applying any logit bias. We then select the candidate that naturally maximizes the watermark score: $y^{*}=\\arg\\max_{y}\\text{Score}_{\\text{wm}}(y)$. This method is “distortion-free” regarding the sampling distribution but is typically infeasible in standard API usage due to cost (generating $L$ times more tokens per chunk).\nL172: #### Entropy-aware detection.\nL173: At detection time, we compute the entropy $H_{t}$ of the model’s predicted distribution at each token position $t$ in the watermarked text $H_{t}=-\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{t}(v)\\log p_{t}(v)$ where $p_{t}(v)$ is the model probability for token $v$ given the prefix up to $t-1$ in the watermarked text. We then apply an entropy filter: a token is included in the watermark score only if $H_{t}$ exceeds a chosen threshold $\\tau$, similar to what is done in cite107†Lee et al. (2023) for code.\nL174: This focuses detection on high-entropy positions, where watermarking is more effective, and ignores low-entropy tokens. All entropy computations are performed on the watermarked text, as the original text is not available at detection time. As a result, these entropy values differ from those at generation-time, when the model was conditioned.\nL175: ## 4 Experiments\nL176: We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of post-hoc watermarking to assess: (1) detection power (the ability to reliably identify watermarked text, measured through $p$-values) and (2) fidelity (the preservation of meaning and quality through paraphrasing). In cite22†subsection 4.1 , we detail the experimental setup, including datasets, models, watermarking schemes, decoding strategies, and evaluation metrics.\nL177: In cite31†subsection 4.2 , we compare watermarking methods on the quality-detectability Pareto frontier, finding that Gumbel-max dominates under standard sampling. In cite32†subsection 4.3 , we show that larger models preserve semantics better, but small/mid-size models offer sweet spots, with stronger watermark signals due to higher entropy. In cite33†subsection 4.4 , we demonstrate that beam search improves the Pareto frontier for all applicable methods.\nL178: In cite34†subsection 4.5 , we evaluate entropy-aware detection and find only modest gains. In cite36†subsection 4.6 , we investigate post-hoc watermarking on code, revealing that correctness constraints limit detectability. In cite40†subsection 4.7 , we evaluate cross-lingual robustness and, in cite41†subsection 4.8 , the impact of chunking on long documents.\nL179: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL180: #### Datasets.\nL181: \nL182: We evaluate on three diverse corpora to capture different text characteristics and use cases:\nL183: \nL184:   * •\nL185: \nL186: Books: Passages in English from the Gutenberg dataset (cite121†Project Gutenberg, 2025 ), containing books. These represent longer-form, literary text with complex sentence structures.\nL187: \nL188:   * •\nL189: \nL190: Wikipedia: Lead paragraphs from 500 randomly sampled Wikipedia articles. These represent factual, encyclopedic text with a formal writing style and different languages.\nL191: \nL192:   * •\nL193: Code: Programming problems from the HumanEval (cite122†Chen et al., 2021 ) and MBPP (cite123†Austin et al., 2021 ) benchmarks, consisting of Python functions with accompanying unit tests.\nL194: #### Paraphrasing models and prompts.\nL195: \nL196: We experiment with several dense open-weight instruct LMs as paraphrasers, spanning different families and sizes to study the impact of model scale on paraphrase quality and watermark preservation. Specifically, we use the instruct version of LLaMA-3 (cite124†Dubey et al., 2024 ) (1B, 3B, 8B, 70B), Gemma-3 (cite125†DeepMind, 2025 ) (1B, 4B, 12B, 27B) , Qwen-2.5 (cite126†Team, 2024 ) (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B, 14B, 32B), and SmolLM-2 (cite127†Allal et al., 2025 ) (135M, 360M, 1.7B).\nL197: To ensure the models outputs the watermarked text directly and without any comments, we use a structured system prompt and prefill the assistant’s response with a prefix (e.g., “Rephrased text:”). An example prompt used is:\nL198: \nL199: “You are a text rephrasing assistant. You must rephrase the given text while strictly preserving its original meaning, style, and structure. You must output only the rephrased text, with no explanations or commentary.”\nL200: #### Chunking strategy.\nL201: \nL202: Large documents may be difficult to rephrase in a single pass, or may even exceed the model’s context window, so we split texts into chunks. We compare two modes: full-context, which processes the entire document at once, and context-aware chunking, which processes each chunk separately while prepending one or two previously rephrased chunks as context to preserve coherence.\nL203: #### Watermarking schemes.\nL204: \nL205: We evaluate multiple watermarking methods (detailed in cite46†subsection A.2 ), all with window size $2$, varying their hyperparameters to explore their detection-quality trade-offs.\nL206: \nL207:   * •\nL208: \nL209: Green-Red List (cite120†Kirchenbauer et al., 2023b ): We vary the bias $\\delta\\in\\{1.0,2.0,4.0\\}$.\nL210: \nL211:   * •\nL212: \nL213: DiPMark (cite114†Wu et al., 2023 ): We vary the reweighting parameter $\\alpha\\in\\{0.2,0.3,0.4\\}$.\nL214: \nL215:   * •\nL216: MorphMark (cite115†Wang et al., 2025 ): We fix $\\kappa=10$ and vary $p_{0}\\in\\{0,0.05,0.1,0.2\\}$.\nL217: \nL218:   * •\nL219: \nL220: SynthID-Text (cite78†Dathathri et al., 2024 ): We vary the number of tournaments $k\\in\\{10,20,30\\}$. We also compute the $p$-values from weighted and non weighted scores as described in cite46†subsection A.2 .\nL221: \nL222:   * •\nL223: \nL224: Gumbel-max (cite76†Aaronson and Kirchner, 2023 ): Fixed by temperature and top-$p$.\nL225: #### Choice of private key.\nL226: We address the private key issue described in cite11†section 3 by testing 50 candidate keys. For each combination of tokenizer, watermarking method (and depth for SynthID), we evaluate the keys on non-watermarked texts to estimate the $p$-value distribution under $H_{0}$. We then select the key that minimizes the deviation from $U(0,1)$, as measured by the Kolmogorov–Smirnov statistic.\nL227: Next, we verify that the theoretical FPRs for these keys match the empirical FPRs on 1.5M Wikipedia documents containing between 1k and 100k tokens (using watermark window deduplication). This ensures that the subsequent results under $H_{1}$ reflect the intrinsic performance of each scheme rather than artifacts of a particular key choice. Further details are provided in cite128†Table 6 .\nL228: #### Entropy aware detection.\nL229: \nL230: For all methods, we evaluate entropy-aware detection by computing per-token entropy with the same model used for rephrasing. At detection, we only score tokens with entropy $\\geq\\tau$, with $\\tau\\in\\{0,0.2,0.4,0.6,\\dots,1.8,2.0\\}$. We note that we only pass the watermarked text to compute the per-token entropy without the original text as context.\nL231: #### Decoding strategies.\nL232: \nL233: We explore different decoding approaches:\nL234: \nL235:   * •\nL236: \nL237: Nucleus sampling (cite129†Holtzman et al., 2019 ). We vary temperature $T\\in\\{0.7,1.0,1.2\\}$ and top-$p\\in\\{0.9,0.95,0.99\\}$. Higher temperature or top-$p$ should increase entropy at the cost of faithfulness.\nL238: \nL239:   * •\nL240: Beam search (cite130†Sutskever et al., 2014 ), as detailed in cite11†section 3 . We test beam widths $B\\in\\{3,5,10\\}$ with same number of candidates per beam. We experiment with selection criteria based on either the non-watermarked likelihood or the watermarked model’s likelihood, and use stochastic (temperature $1$ and top-$p$ 0.95) or deterministic selection within beams.\nL241: \nL242:   * •\nL243: WaterMax (cite116†Giboulot and Furon, 2024 ), as detailed in cite11†section 3 . We generate $L\\in\\{4,8,16\\}$ non-watermarked candidate tokens per draft $m\\in\\{4,8\\}$ and select the one with the highest number of green tokens, and repeat.\nL244: #### Evaluation metrics.\nL245: \nL246: We employ the following metrics to evaluate performance:\nL247: \nL248:   * •\nL249: \nL250: Detection: We compute the $p$-value of the corresponding detection test, which can be read as the probability that a watermark score at least as high would happen if the text was not watermarked.\nL251: \nL252:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPixel Seal: Adversarial-only training for invisible image and video watermarking (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16874v1)\nciteturn6471view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6465view1\",\"lineno\":128}); Total lines: 408\nL117: Ensuring that a watermark remains invisible in the content is the primary challenge in post-hoc watermarking research. For example, cite50†Bui et al. (2023) utilize four different losses specifically designed to make watermarks invisible. However, many such loss functions have modes in which the loss value is small, but the watermark is very visible to humans. Therefore, making the watermark invisible requires careful hyperparameter tuning without any guarantees of success.\nL118: And even with carefully tuned hyperparameters, watermarks often remain visible in flat areas as waves (cite50†Bui et al., 2023 ) or blobs (cite78†Fernandez et al., 2024 ).\nL119: In the attempt to solve the imperceptibility issue, in contrast to previous work, as shown in Figure cite86†2 , we remove all perceptual losses and train the watermarking model using only the cross-entropy message loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{msg}}$ and an adversarial loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}(x,x_{w})$:\nL120: \nL121:  | $$\\frac{1}{2}\\nabla_{\\theta_{D}}\\Bigl[\\text{ReLU}\\bigl(1-D(x)\\bigr)+\\text{ReLU}\\bigl(1+D(x_{w})\\bigr)\\Bigr]-\\lambda_{adv}\\nabla_{\\theta_{\\text{Emb}}}D(x_{w})$$  |  | (6)\nL122: where $x$ and $x_{w}$ are the original and watermarked images, $D$ is a patch-based discriminator network, $\\theta_{D}$ its parameters, and $\\theta_{\\text{Emb}}$ are the parameters of the watermark embedder. The architecture of the discriminator affects how effectively it can remove visible artifacts. The more effective the discriminator is in removing visible artifacts, the less robust the final watermark is to various content modifications.\nL123: In this work, we chose the patch-based discriminator of cite26†Rombach et al. (2022) as it provided a good balance.\nL124: To control the imperceptibility of the watermark, we introduce the watermark boosting technique. The watermark boosting artificially amplifies the artifacts that are present in a watermarked image, making them easier for the discriminator to be detected and removed. In detail, the boosted watermarked image $\\hat{x}_{w}$, computed as $\\hat{x}_{w}=x+\\beta(x_{w}-x)$, replaces the regular watermarked image $x_{w}$ in Equation (cite87†6 ).\nL125: Setting $\\beta>1$ ensures greater imperceptibility of the watermark, while $\\beta<1$ allows for very robust watermarks.\nL126: While the adversarial loss ensures the watermarked image does not contain any noticeable unnatural artifacts, the watermarked image can still significantly deviate from the original reference image.\nL127: This is a lesser issue for watermarking of AI-generated content, where users are not provided with the original reference image, but it can be a major challenge for other provenance applications (cite30†Castro, 2025 ), such as professional photography devices, where the watermark must be minimal (ideally comparable to camera noise), so as not to alter the user’s intent.\nL128: To restrict the allowed deviation the watermark $w$ can introduce to the original content, we reduce the watermark magnitude by a global scaling factor $\\alpha$ and a local Just Noticeable Difference (cite88†Zhang et al., 2008 ) attenuation map as done by cite55†Sander et al. (2025) .\nL129: The attenuation map is computed using a non-learnable function $\\text{JND}:[0,1]^{H\\times W\\times 3}\\to[0,1]^{H\\times W}$ which assigns large values for pixels where changes to pixel intensities will likely remain unnoticed by humans (e.g., edges) and small values otherwise. The watermarked image $x_{w}$ is therefore computed as follows:\nL130:  | $$x_{w}=x+\\alpha\\cdot\\left(w\\odot\\text{JND}(x)\\right),\\quad w=\\text{Emb}(x,m).$$  |  | (7)\nL131: cite89†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Training and inference of Pixel Seal on high-resolution images. The input image is first resized to the model resolution (256$\\times$256) and the raw watermark is computed using the Pixel Seal embedder. Then, this watermark is resized to the original input resolution and pixel-wise multiplied by the Just-Noticeable Difference (JND) map to obtain the final high-resolution watermark.\nL132: ### 4.2 Three-stage training schedule\nL133: Training the watermark embedder and extractor can be very brittle and sensitive to hyperparameters, as global optima of perceptual and adversarial losses correspond to an empty watermark $x_{w}=x$ ($w=\\bm{0}$). Related works stabilize the training process by reducing the weight of perceptual losses $\\lambda_{\\text{perc}}$ at the beginning of the training. cite51†Xu et al. (2025) even delay the application of augmentations $\\tau$ to the later stages of training to improve the convergence of the model.\nL134: Rather than carefully tuning hyperparameters, we observe a three-stage training can resolve the instabilities and allow the model to repeatably converge to a similar solution.\nL135: In detail, in the first stage, the model is trained only with the message reconstruction loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{msg}}$ and the watermark scaling factor is set to a large value $\\alpha=\\alpha_{0}$, as done by cite55†Sander et al. (2025) . At this stage, the predicted watermark is highly visible, yet it facilitates a stable learning process. We train the model until the bit accuracy is saturated, after which we move to the next stage.\nL136: In the second stage, we add the adversarial loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}$ and we gradually decrease the watermark scaling factor $\\alpha$ from the initial value $\\alpha_{0}$ to the final value $\\alpha_{1}$ using a cosine schedule. We use the following schedule:\nL137:  | $$\\alpha(t)=\\alpha_{1}+(\\alpha_{0}-\\alpha_{1})\\cdot\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\varphi\\right),\\quad\\varphi=\\mathrm{clip}\\left(\\frac{t-N_{start}}{N_{end}-N_{start}},0,1\\right).$$  |  | (8)\nL138: The factor $\\varphi$ controls the interpolation schedule based on the current epoch $t$, the second stage start epoch $N_{start}$, and the second stage end epoch $N_{end}$. The second-stage training ensures that the resulting watermark is imperceptible while still being robust against various attacks.\nL139: In the final stage, we finetune the model with the final watermark scaling factor value $\\alpha=\\alpha_{1}$, potentially using different types of data, attacks, etc., to tailor the watermarking model for any specific application needs.\nL140: ### 4.3 High-resolution adaptation\nL141: Computing a watermark directly on a high-resolution image with millions of pixels is computationally prohibitively expensive. Therefore, a common approach is to train a watermark embedder and extractor using a fixed spatial resolution, e.g., 256$\\times$256, and use watermark interpolation technique (cite50†Bui et al., 2023 ; cite55†Sander et al., 2025 ) for inference.\nL142: The technique works by downsampling the original image $x$ to the fixed model resolution using bilinear interpolation and later upsampling the low-resolution watermark $w$ back to the original resolution as shown in Equation (cite90†9 ).\nL143:  | $$x_{w}=x+\\alpha\\cdot\\text{resize}_{\\uparrow}(w),\\quad w=\\text{Emb}\\left(\\text{resize}_{\\downarrow}(x),m\\right)$$  |  | (9)\nL144: However, this approach has three major downsides. First, the watermark upsampling can often result in artifacts being spilled into regions where they are more visible (e.g., areas around edges). Second, some artifacts that are imperceptible in low resolution can be easily spotted in high resolution.\nL145: And third, the watermark extractor is trained to extract hidden messages from images $\\tilde{x}_{w}=\\text{resize}_{\\downarrow}(x)+\\alpha\\cdot w$, where both $\\tilde{x}_{w}$ and $w$ are in the fixed model resolution, while during inference the extractor detects the hidden message in image $\\tilde{x}^{\\prime}_{w}=\\text{resize}_{\\downarrow}(x+\\alpha\\cdot\\text{resize}_{\\uparrow}(w))$.\nL146: Because in the general case $\\tilde{x}_{w}$ and $\\tilde{x}^{\\prime}_{w}$ are not equal, the watermark extractor is tasked with detecting watermarks in out-of-distribution data.\nL147: We address these challenges as follows: First, to address the spill of watermark artifacts into flat regions where they are more visible, we compute the JND attenuation map in the original image resolution. Although this increases the computational requirements during inference, the increase is substantially lower than the computation of the watermark in higher resolution.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPixel Seal: Adversarial-only training for invisible image and video watermarking (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16874v1)\nciteturn6471view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6465view1\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 408\nL173: During the fist stage training we set the watermark scaling factor to $\\alpha_{0}=1.0$ and decrease it during the second stage to the final value of $\\alpha_{1}=0.2$. The released Pixel Seal model is trained with the discriminator of cite26†Rombach et al. (2022) with the default watermark boosting factor $\\beta=1$. The objectives are weighted with $\\lambda_{\\text{msg}}=1.0$ and $\\lambda_{\\text{adv}}=0.1$. We do not use any perceptual loss. Our models encode $n_{\\text{bits}}=256$ bits.\nL174: Baselines. We compare Pixel Seal with other publicly available multi-bit watermarking methods.\nL175: We consider CIN (cite48†Ma et al., 2022 ) with 30-bit message, InvisMark (cite51†Xu et al., 2025 ) with 94-bit message^{1}^{1} 1 InvisMark encodes 100-bit message; however, due to its random message sampling process during training, 6 bits of the message have their values fixed., MBRS (cite49†Jia et al., 2021 ) with 256-bit message, TrustMark (cite50†Bui et al., 2023 ) with 100-bit message, Video Seal 0.0 (cite78†Fernandez et al., 2024 ) with 96-bit message, Video Seal 1.0 with 256-bit message, and WAM (cite55†Sander et al., 2025 ) with 32-bit message.\nL176: For video watermarking, we also compare with RivaGAN (cite69†Zhang et al., 2019 ) with 32 32-bit message. Similarly to Pixel Seal, the baselines also operate at a fixed resolution, i.e., we use Equation (cite90†9 ) when evaluating them on high-resolution content.\nL177: Evaluation details. We evaluate the methods using 1000 images of resolution 1280$\\times$1280 generated by Meta AI using the prompts from cite57†Gowal et al. (2025) and 100 real high-resolution photos from the SA-1b validation set (cite116†Kirillov et al., 2023 ). For video evaluation, we further use a dataset of 121 videos^{2}^{2} 2 The videos are available at cite117†www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL86eLlsPNfyi27GSizYjinpYxp7gEl5K8†www.youtube.com .\nL178: generated by Movie Gen (cite29†Polyak et al., 2024 ) and an SA-V validation set of 96 high-quality videos. The evaluation is performed on the first 3 seconds of each video. We measure both watermark imperceptibility and robustness. For imperceptibility, we use PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS (cite80†Zhang et al., 2018 ), CVVDP (cite118†Mantiuk et al., 2024 ), and our just-noticeable difference (JND) metric.\nL179: The JND metric is computed using a regressor trained on internal user study data to measure the perceptual quality difference between the watermarked image and the reference. A difference of 1 JND means that 75% of observers would find the difference noticeable^{3}^{3} 3 Please note that the JND metric described here differs from the JND heatmap used to attenuate the watermark on flat areas..\nL180: For evaluating watermark robustness, we apply various transformations (e.g., brightness change, cropping, compression) and report both the bit accuracy and $\\log_{10}p$. The latter represents the logarithm of the probability of observing the measured bit accuracy by chance (Equation (cite79†2 )). This allows for a fair comparison between methods with different payload sizes (we refer to cite78†Fernandez et al. (2024, App. A) for more information on $\\log_{10}p$).\nL181: The full list of evaluated transformations, along with visual examples, is available in the appendix.\nL182: Table 1: Robustness evaluation. We evaluate the robustness of all methods under various attacks on image (SA-1b and Meta AI images) and video datasets (MovieGen and SA-V videos) using the original content resolution. In addition to the bit accuracy, we report negative $\\log_{10}p$ to account for the different number of bits encoded by each method. Pixel Seal constantly outperforms all related methods, especially in the case of difficult geometric and combined attacks.\nL183: The image and video attacks differ; the full list of attacks is available in the appendix.\nL184:  |  | Identity  | Valuemetric  | Compression  | Geometric  | Combined\nL185:  |  | Bit acc. ($\\uparrow$)/$-\\log_{10}p$ ($\\uparrow$)  | Bit acc. ($\\uparrow$)/$-\\log_{10}p$ ($\\uparrow$)  | Bit acc. ($\\uparrow$)/$-\\log_{10}p$ ($\\uparrow$)  | Bit acc. ($\\uparrow$)/$-\\log_{10}p$ ($\\uparrow$)  | Bit acc. ($\\uparrow$)/$-\\log_{10}p$ ($\\uparrow$)\nL186: Meta AI (images)  | CIN  | $1.00$  | $9.0$  | $0.85$  | $7.5$  | $1.00$  | $9.0$  | $0.52$  | $0.7$  | $0.50$  | $0.4$\nL187: InvisMark  | $1.00$  | $28.3$  | $0.89$  | $22.0$  | $0.98$  | $26.1$  | $0.77$  | $14.8$  | $0.94$  | $22.1$\nL188: MBRS  | $1.00$  | 75.9  | $0.95$  | $62.0$  | $1.00$  | 75.2  | $0.52$  | $3.6$  | $0.50$  | $0.4$\nL189: TrustMark-P  | $1.00$  | $29.6$  | $0.89$  | $21.2$  | $0.98$  | $27.1$  | $0.65$  | $8.2$  | $0.52$  | $0.7$\nL190: TrustMark-Q  | $1.00$  | $30.0$  | $0.97$  | $27.1$  | $1.00$  | $29.8$  | $0.67$  | $9.4$  | $0.54$  | $0.9$\nL191: Video Seal 0.0  | $0.97$  | $25.0$  | $0.84$  | $16.7$  | $0.93$  | $20.9$  | $0.80$  | $13.3$  | $0.74$  | $7.4$\nL192: Video Seal 1.0  | $0.98$  | $69.4$  | $0.95$  | $60.7$  | $0.95$  | $60.6$  | $0.83$  | $42.9$  | $0.69$  | $16.0$\nL193: WAM  | $1.00$  | $9.6$  | $0.89$  | $7.5$  | $1.00$  | $9.5$  | $0.77$  | $4.7$  | $0.62$  | $1.6$\nL194: Pixel Seal (ours)  | $1.00$  | $75.8$  | $0.97$  | 68.1  | $0.98$  | $68.9$  | $0.95$  | 63.9  | $0.91$  | 50.3\nL195: SA-1b (photos)  | CIN  | $1.00$  | $9.0$  | $0.85$  | $7.5$  | $1.00$  | $9.0$  | $0.52$  | $0.7$  | $0.50$  | $0.4$\nL196: InvisMark  | $1.00$  | $28.3$  | $0.89$  | $21.4$  | $1.00$  | $27.8$  | $0.77$  | $14.1$  | $0.98$  | $25.7$\nL197: MBRS  | $0.99$  | $71.8$  | $0.93$  | $55.9$  | $0.99$  | $71.1$  | $0.52$  | $3.4$  | $0.50$  | $0.4$\nL198: TrustMark-P  | $0.99$  | $28.8$  | $0.84$  | $17.3$  | $0.98$  | $26.7$  | $0.64$  | $7.7$  | $0.52$  | $0.6$\nL199: TrustMark-Q  | $1.00$  | $29.9$  | $0.96$  | $25.9$  | $1.00$  | $29.7$  | $0.65$  | $8.5$  | $0.53$